  "speaker": "Abhinavagupta (commentator voice)",
  "question": "This is the very opening of the IPVV. The maṅgala (the prostration to Śiva) has been\nmade; now Abhinavagupta expounds the first kārikā — the *spontaneity* (svācchandya) of the Lord. His\nthesis: the Lord is not a bare, featureless light that reveals a world outside itself; he is the\nself-aware manifestation, the very being of which is to shine forth as the world and to be aware of\nshining. The whole of his doctrine (the svātantryavāda) is here in seed.",
  "premises": [
   "The blue and the other things, without abandoning their blue-ness, shine forth and are one with the\nmanifestation (prakāśābhedamayāḥ). This is the Lord's spontaneity (svācchandya): being of the nature of\nconsciousness (saṃvidātmaka), he shines forth as the varied knowers-and-knowns, with a body that\nappears as more than the consciousness-form even though it is not more, and he is aware of (vimṛśati)\nthis. The shining of the not-more as if it were more is the \"clear\" (svaccha) nature, like a mirror: the\nmirror reflects the wall, the house, the elephant, the horse, the person, the pot, the potter — all the\ndistinctions of parts and part-holders, support and supported, cause and effect — not being more than\nitself, yet appearing as if it were. Its not being aware of this (tathāvimarśābhāvād) is its\nun-spontaneity, its inertia (jaḍatva). But the param-eśvara, shining thus, *is aware* of himself as\nshining — for the consciousness culminates in the reflexive-awareness (saṃvido vimarśaparyantatvāt).",
   "The objection: \"then it would always shine so.\" — The \"always\" (śaśvat): if \"sometimes\" were a real,\nindependent time, it might be objected; but there is no such time. \"At this time\" — the word's meaning\nis nothing other than the sun's movement (the shining sun). So the question \"why does the world shine\nwith the sun's movement?\" is like asking \"why does not the blue shine as yellow?\" And if it did not,\nthe sun's movement would not shine either. On the day of dissolution, that very day would be today, not\nthe present day. So there is nothing un-experiential here. The consciousness's non-excess is seen even\nin the dream, the construction, and the making of a śāstra — so the fabrication beyond the experience\nis the un-experiential. Hence: \"śaśvat\" = the impossibility of time-division; \"acchanna\" = the\nnon-excess of the own-form; \"svaccha\" = the possibility of the excess-appearance even in the\nnon-excess; \"svacchanda\" = the union with the freedom of the reflexive-awareness.",
   "The vṛtti-word shows the identity-manifestation of these forms in the Lord, the powerful Śiva: śaśvat\n(un-cut by time), acchanna (not partaking of the other-ness of the own-form in the least), svacchā\n(bearing the shining of the more), svacchandā (free of the other-rest-dependence, the self-resting\nspanda). He, Śiva, the param-eśvara, is the param-essence of the supreme-and-lower good (the bliss-juice\nenjoyment and liberation), who removes the saṃsāric delusion of the a-śiva form — to him the śāstra-kṛt\nmakes the prostration. The prostration is the samāveśa (the entry): the abandoning of the\nmāyā-knower's conceit, the subordination of the \"I\"-conceit, and the rising of the consciousness-form\nown-nature. The \"namaḥ\" is a mere indicator (like the other-nipāta), not the offering of a word-form;\nby the Gītā-rule, the \"namaḥ\" heard pleases the Lord. The svāhā and the rest, in the offering, have\nonly the indicated meaning (the increase of the flame, the gift to the deity) — not a bare word-form."
  ],
  "inference": "Decision for L2\nProceed. The argument is understood: the Lord's spontaneity is the self-aware manifestation — the\nconsciousness that shines as the world and is aware of shining (like a mirror, but aware). The four\nepithets (un-cut by time, un-other, clear, spontaneous) are unpacked. The prostration is the entry into\nthis self-aware Śiva. The whole doctrine is here in seed.",
